WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.660
Jess wants two hundred ribbons of length 110 centimeters for a party

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:13.258
However, the ribbons were sold at 25 meters per tape how many tapes will Jess need?

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:20.399
This problem is sparking controversy because it was given to primary school students in Singapore.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.090
However, many adults could not solve this problem and felt

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.689
it was too tricky to be given to 12 year old students.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:39.179
Can you figure it out? Give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching the video for the solution.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.329
While this problem is not too difficult,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:55.439
it is fairly tricky, and I myself came up with the wrong answer by using the wrong approach.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:02.069
The first pitfall is that the problem involves multiple units of measurement.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.040
So we need to make sure to use only one unit of measurement. I will convert everything to meters.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:10.740
The first step:

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:18.299
I'll say that the hundred and ten centimeters of each ribbon equals one point one meters for each ribbon

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.939
Next

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:28.139
I multiplied the one point one meters by the two hundred ribbons that were needed to get the total length of

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.930
220 meters.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:37.199
I then took that total length and divided by the 25 meters per tape to get that there would be

00:01:37.659 --> 00:01:40.649
8.8 tapes needed to produce that length.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:46.320
I then said since eight tapes are too few, we would need nine tapes in total.

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:48.869
But this is the wrong answer.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:51.599
In fact, 10 tapes are needed.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.540
Why is that?

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:59.789
The reason is we shouldn't be considering just the total length of ribbon,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.719
but we need to consider how much ribbon is used per tape.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:07.769
Each tape has 25 meters

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:15.830
So we need to take the 25 meters per tape and divide it by the 1.1 meters per ribbon

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:22.190
We end up that each tape can produce 22 whole ribbons plus an excess amount.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.599
But this is not a whole ribbon amount; it's less than one.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:36.319
So each tape can make 22 whole ribbons, and then we're gonna have some excess material, which is going to be wasted.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.559
Now technically you could try and combine this excess material between the tapes,

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:48.469
but nowhere in the problem does it say that you can do that. So you'd likely have to assume that the excess material

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.769
goes wasted. So you would have to say that each tape can produce 22 whole ribbons.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.299
From there,

00:02:57.299 --> 00:03:03.709
we know that we need 200 total ribbons so we divide that by the 22 ribbons per tape, to get that we'll need

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:06.828
9 + 1/11 tapes.

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:08.730
In other words,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.299
we'll need more than 9 tapes since

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:16.819
9 tapes are too few, we will need 10 tapes to produce all the 200 ribbons.

00:03:19.079 --> 00:03:21.469
So, this is a fairly tricky question, and

00:03:22.109 --> 00:03:29.239
getting to the correct answer of 10 tapes requires some thoughtful process of considering the dimensions and the

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.970
excess material of each tape.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.450
Did you figure out this problem?

